        content:
          "Before any risk can be assessed, it has to be identified — and oil and gas sites present a genuinely wide range of hazard categories, often several at once in the same location. This lesson works through the main categories you'll be trained to recognize.\n\n## Physical Hazards\n\nPhysical hazards include noise from rotating equipment and compressors, vibration, extreme heat from flaring or equipment, working at height on rigs and platforms, and moving machinery. On a drilling rig or production platform, physical hazards are often the most visible category, but not always the most dangerous — that distinction usually belongs to process safety hazards.\n\n## Process Safety Hazards\n\nThese are hazards tied to the loss of containment of hazardous materials — a pipe failure, a valve that doesn't hold, a pressure vessel exceeding its limits. Process safety hazards are what turn routine operations into major incidents: fires, explosions, and toxic releases almost always trace back to a process safety failure somewhere in the chain. This category gets its own dedicated attention across the industry precisely because when it goes wrong, it tends to go wrong catastrophically rather than in small, contained ways.\n\n## Chemical Hazards\n\nHydrocarbons themselves, along with drilling fluids, produced water, and hydrogen sulfide (H2S) gas, which is both toxic and, at certain concentrations, undetectable by smell despite its usual rotten-egg odor at lower concentrations. H2S specifically is treated as one of the most serious chemical hazards in upstream operations, because exposure at high concentrations can be fatal within minutes, and because the sense you'd normally rely on to detect it — smell — actually fails you at the most dangerous concentrations.\n\n## Ergonomic and Psychosocial Hazards\n\nRepetitive manual tasks, awkward postures, and heavy lifting fall under ergonomic hazards — genuinely serious over a career, even though they rarely make headlines the way an explosion does. Psychosocial hazards cover fatigue from long shift rotations, isolation on remote or offshore postings, and the cumulative stress of working in a high-consequence environment. Modern HSE programs increasingly treat these as seriously as physical hazards, since fatigue in particular is a documented contributing factor in a significant share of serious incidents.\n\n## Bringing It Together\n\nThis lesson worked through five hazard categories present on oil and gas sites: physical, process safety, chemical, ergonomic, and psychosocial. Recognizing which category a given hazard falls into is the first step toward managing it correctly — a process safety hazard needs a fundamentally different response than an ergonomic one. The next lesson takes this list of hazard types and applies a formal method for assessing how much risk each one actually represents.",
