        content:
          "Identifying a hazard is only the first step. The next is figuring out how seriously to take it — and that's what Hazard Identification and Risk Assessment, universally shortened to **HIRA**, is built to do.\n\n## The Core HIRA Process\n\nHIRA runs through a repeatable sequence: identify the hazards present in a task or area, determine who could be harmed and how, evaluate the risk by considering both likelihood and severity, decide on control measures, and record the findings for review. This isn't a one-time exercise — a proper HIRA gets revisited whenever the task, the equipment, or the environment changes.\n\n## The Risk Matrix\n\nMost HIRA processes score risk using a matrix that plots **likelihood** against **severity**. Likelihood might range from rare to almost certain; severity might range from minor injury to multiple fatalities. Where a hazard lands on that matrix determines how urgently it needs to be controlled and who has the authority to accept the residual risk. A hazard with high likelihood and high severity — say, a poorly maintained high-pressure line near a populated work area — demands immediate action, often by someone senior enough to authorize stopping work entirely. A hazard with low likelihood and low severity might simply get logged and monitored.\n\n## The Hierarchy of Controls\n\nOnce a risk is assessed, HIRA doesn't stop at 'be careful' — it applies a structured hierarchy of controls, from most to least effective: **elimination** (removing the hazard entirely), **substitution** (replacing it with something less hazardous), **engineering controls** (physically isolating people from the hazard, like a guard rail or a containment system), **administrative controls** (procedures, permits, training, signage), and **PPE** (personal protective equipment) as the last line of defense, not the first. A common mistake among newer workers is reaching straight for PPE as the solution to every hazard, when PPE is actually meant to catch whatever risk remains after the more effective controls above it have already been applied.\n\n## Why This Matters for You as a Candidate\n\nBeing able to talk through a HIRA process — not just recite the acronym, but actually walk through identifying a hazard, placing it on a risk matrix, and proposing a control using the hierarchy above — is one of the most concrete, demonstrable HSE skills you can bring into a graduate-trainee interview. It's a structured way of thinking, and structured thinking is exactly what this kind of interview is trying to screen for.\n\n## Bringing It Together\n\nThis lesson covered the HIRA process end to end: identifying hazards, scoring them on a risk matrix by likelihood and severity, and applying the hierarchy of controls from elimination down to PPE. Next week turns from identifying and assessing risk to the legal framework that actually requires companies to do this work in the first place — starting with the regulatory bodies that oversee it in Nigeria specifically.",
